  };

  The second argument is what makes signup's confirm-password work with no special case:

  [36mconfirmPassword[39m: [[33mrequired[39m(), [33mmatches[39m([31m'password'[39m, [31m'Passwords must match'[39m)],

  Every rule takes an optional custom message as its last argument, defaulting to a sensible one.

  [1mOne convention worth stating explicitly:[0m every rule [3mexcept[0m [38;5;153mrequired[39m passes on an empty value. Without that, a blank
  required field fires [38;5;153mrequired[39m [3mand[0m [38;5;153mminLength[39m and the user sees two complaints about one mistake. It also means
  "optional but must be valid if present" is just a schema that omits [38;5;153mrequired()[39m.

  [1m[38;5;153mvalidate(values, schema)[0m returns [38;5;153m{ valid, errors }[39m, where [38;5;153merrors[39m maps field name → the [3mfirst[0m failing rule's message.
  First-error-wins rather than collecting all of them: only one message per field gets displayed, and showing "required"
  and "too short" together is noise. Rule order in the array is therefore meaningful — cheapest/most fundamental first.

  [1m[38;5;153mbindForm(formEl, schema, onValid, options)[0m — on submit it prevents the default, reads values from the form's inputs,
  validates, and either renders errors or clears them and calls [38;5;153monValid(values)[39m. Login's [38;5;153mlogin()[39m call moves into that
  callback unchanged.

  Three details in it:

  - [1mIt reads inputs by [38;5;153mname[39m.[0m The current inputs have only [38;5;153mid[39m, so I'd add [38;5;153mname[39m attributes — that's also what makes the
    form work correctly as a form.
  - [1mWhen it validates:[0m on submit, and after the [3mfirst[0m failed submit a field re-validates as you type, so errors clear as
    they're fixed. It won't nag you with errors before you've submitted once.
  - [1mHow errors render:[0m for field [38;5;153mx[39m, it fills [38;5;153m[data-error-for="x"][39m inside the form, and sets [38;5;153maria-invalid[39m /
    [38;5;153maria-describedby[39m on the input so screen readers get it too. Form-level errors go to [38;5;153m[data-error-for="_form"][39m.
    [38;5;153moptions.renderErrors[39m overrides the whole display strategy for a form that wants something else.

  [38;5;153mvalidate()[39m remains usable on its own, so a form that doesn't want any of [38;5;153mbindForm[39m's opinions can skip it.

  Does the API look right — particularly first-error-wins and the "only [38;5;153mrequired[39m fires on empty" convention?

